ening right now so  so there's a social media campaign uh  we've also sent out uh uh direct direct  mail to individuals to say hey this is  you know it's time to get your uh to get  your booster uh so uh you can  see you can see it online and we can  also send some of the materials to you  as well  right thanks for that  so we'll take one last caller please  thank you catherine grockowski alberta  today  both uh thanks minister you had  mentioned  that we need to stop  making people work overtime  you mentioned the strain on ems and  today  hsaa president mike parker  had given three suggestions for for the  ems workforce including  hiring casual employees to full time  um  [Music]  not forcing overtime  is there any  desire to take take  the hsaa up on those suggestions to  address the strain on ems  well i think uh  mr parker for his suggestions you know  we we recognize that there is a  challenge uh with with ems and the the  call volume has increased significantly  we can track it since uh uh since last  summer uh to address you know this in  part we um you know ahs uh announced a  10-point plan and actually i was pleased  you know yesterday they just launched a  uh  integrated operating center  for dispatch uh  undergo on top of dispatch and here in  calgary they have an ioc in edmonton to  be to be able to do better load leveling  and and be able to move patients through  the system and send it to different area  hospitals and uh uh urgent care centers  uh so that you know that was one part of  the the ten point plan uh in addition  you know budget 2022 we're committing an  additional 64 million dollars  to ems across the province which  includes you know in calgary and  edmonton additional resources right to  to reduce the the need to actually call  upon um  our frontline workers to to to do over  time to be able to uh deal with the  surge and then lastly uh we also  appointed a uh advisory committee to  deal with ems which uh which includes mr  parker and part of this advisory  committee has a bunch of subtables so  they're looking at ways that they can  you know improve you know rules around  dispatch to keep  ambulances in their home territory  longer how do we address the issues of  wait times that ambulance i have at the  hospitals which have been challenging  particularly right now given the huge  volumes that are coming in um that we're  seeing we're seeing that grow  but looking at different ways we can  approach that and then one one of those  tables is also looking at  the  recruitment and ret retention and  training of of of ems  recognizing that you know  this is a stressful job how do we  actually uh make it less stressful um  you know looking at work fatigue so  there's another initiative part of that  looking at work for work fatigue i'm  really looking forward to the committee  uh in which mr parker is a member to  making recommendations to me i've asked  them to make you know if there's any  quick recommendations that that we can  make that the committee agrees with that  say hey this can actually make a  difference real time um and not reduce  uh this safety then then let's try them  out so i i expect to see those uh like  in the very within uh very short order  like within the next week uh  additionally there will be an interim  report that's coming the next few weeks  um on that and then and then a longer  term report in the fall so i i fully  appreciate those issues here i thank mr  parker for the suggestion um and as a  member of the committee to be able to  work with committee members to say you  know what can we do right now to uh to  address this that we think will all work  and then let's try to  sorry minister it sounds like your mike  went dead at least here in edmonton i  think we only missed the last few  seconds so we'll go on if that's okay  with you  i catherine i i think we owe you a  follow-up but just before that uh aside  uh some of the specific questions are  really for ahs as the e